.TH "frontend/node_modules/postcss-load-config Directory Reference" 3 "Version 0.1" "Chat-ME!" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/postcss-load-config Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
  \fR \fP  
.SH "Load Config"
.PP

.PP
 

.PP
.SS "Install"

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm i \-D postcss\-load\-config
.fi
.PP

.PP
.SS "Usage"

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm i \-S|\-D postcss\-plugin
.fi
.PP

.PP
Install all required PostCSS plugins and save them to your \fBpackage\&.json\fP \fRdependencies\fP/\fRdevDependencies\fP

.PP
Then create a PostCSS config file by choosing one of the following formats
.SS "\fRpackage\&.json\fP"
Create a \fB\fRpostcss\fP\fP section in your project's \fB\fRpackage\&.json\fP\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
Project (Root)
  |– client
  |– public
  |
  |\- package\&.json
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "postcss": {
    "parser": "sugarss",
    "map": false,
    "plugins": {
      "postcss\-plugin": {}
    }
  }
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.postcssrc\fP"
Create a \fB\fR\&.postcssrc\fP\fP file in JSON or YAML format

.PP
.RS 4
ℹ️ It's recommended to use an extension (e\&.g \fB\fR\&.postcssrc\&.json\fP\fP or \fB\fR\&.postcssrc\&.yml\fP\fP) instead of \fR\&.postcssrc\fP 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
Project (Root)
  |– client
  |– public
  |
  |\- (\&.postcssrc|\&.postcssrc\&.json|\&.postcssrc\&.yml)
  |\- package\&.json
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fB\fR\&.postcssrc\&.json\fP\fP 
.PP
.nf
{
  "parser": "sugarss",
  "map": false,
  "plugins": {
    "postcss\-plugin": {}
  }
}

.fi
.PP

.PP
\fB\fR\&.postcssrc\&.yml\fP\fP 
.PP
.nf
parser: sugarss
map: false
plugins:
  postcss\-plugin: {}

.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
For YAML configs, you must have \fRyaml\fP installed as a peer dependency\&.
.RE
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP or \fRpostcss\&.config\&.js\fP"
You may need some logic within your config\&. In this case create JS/TS file named:
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.postcssrc\&.mjs\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.postcssrc\&.cjs\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.postcssrc\&.ts\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.postcssrc\&.mts\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\&.postcssrc\&.cts\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpostcss\&.config\&.js\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpostcss\&.config\&.mjs\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpostcss\&.config\&.cjs\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpostcss\&.config\&.ts\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpostcss\&.config\&.mts\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpostcss\&.config\&.cts\fP
.PP

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
For TypeScript configs, you must have \fRtsx\fP or \fRjiti\fP installed as a peer dependency\&.
.RE
.PP
.PP
.nf
Project (Root)
  |– client
  |– public
  |\- (\&.postcssrc|postcss\&.config)\&.(js|mjs|cjs|ts|mts|cts)
  |\- package\&.json
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can export the config as an \fR{Object}\fP

.PP
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  parser: 'sugarss',
  map: false,
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-plugin': {}
  }
}

.fi
.PP

.PP
Or export a \fR{Function}\fP that returns the config (more about the \fRctx\fP param below)

.PP
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = (ctx) => ({
  parser: ctx\&.parser ? 'sugarss' : false,
  map: ctx\&.env === 'development' ? ctx\&.map : false,
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-plugin': ctx\&.options\&.plugin
  }
})

.fi
.PP

.PP
Plugins can be loaded either using an \fR{Object}\fP or an \fR{Array}\fP
.SS "\fR{Object}\fP"
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = ({ env }) => ({
  \&.\&.\&.options,
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-plugin': env === 'production' ? {} : false
  }
})

.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
ℹ️ When using an \fR{Object}\fP, the key can be a Node\&.js module name, a path to a JavaScript file that is relative to the directory of the PostCSS config file, or an absolute path to a JavaScript file\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "\fR{Array}\fP"
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = ({ env }) => ({
  \&.\&.\&.options,
  plugins: [
    env === 'production' ? require('postcss\-plugin')() : false
  ]
})

.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.RS 4
:warning: When using an \fR{Array}\fP, make sure to \fRrequire()\fP each plugin 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.SS "Options"

.PP
Name  Type  Default  Description  \fB**`to`**\fP  \fR{String}\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Destination File Path  \fB**`map`**\fP  \fR{String\\|Object}\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Enable/Disable Source Maps  \fB**`from`**\fP  \fR{String}\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Source File Path  \fB**`parser`**\fP  \fR{String\\|Function}\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Custom PostCSS Parser  \fB**`syntax`**\fP  \fR{String\\|Function}\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Custom PostCSS Syntax  \fB**`stringifier`**\fP  \fR{String\\|Function}\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Custom PostCSS Stringifier  
.SS "\fRparser\fP"
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  parser: 'sugarss'
}

.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRsyntax\fP"
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  syntax: 'postcss\-scss'
}

.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRstringifier\fP"
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  stringifier: 'midas'
}

.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\fB\fRmap\fP\fP\fP"
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  map: 'inline'
}

.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:warning: In most cases \fRoptions\&.from\fP && \fRoptions\&.to\fP are set by the third-party which integrates this package (CLI, gulp, webpack)\&. It's unlikely one needs to set/use \fRoptions\&.from\fP && \fRoptions\&.to\fP within a config file\&. Unless you're a third-party plugin author using this module and its Node API directly \fBdont't set \fRoptions\&.from\fP && \fRoptions\&.to\fP yourself\fP 
.RE
.PP
.SS "\fRto\fP"
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  to: 'path/to/dest\&.css'
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRfrom\fP"
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  from: 'path/to/src\&.css'
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.SS "Plugins"
.SS "\fR{} || null\fP"
The plugin will be loaded with defaults

.PP
.PP
.nf
'postcss\-plugin': {} || null
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-plugin': {} || null
  }
}

.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:warning: \fR{}\fP must be an \fBempty\fP \fR{Object}\fP literal 
.RE
.PP
.SS "\fR{Object}\fP"
The plugin will be loaded with given options

.PP
.PP
.nf
'postcss\-plugin': { option: '', option: '' }
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-plugin': { option: '', option: '' }
  }
}

.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRfalse\fP"
The plugin will not be loaded

.PP
.PP
.nf
'postcss\-plugin': false
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fB\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = {
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-plugin': false
  }
}

.fi
.PP
.SS "\fROrdering\fP"
Plugin \fBexecution order\fP is determined by declaration in the plugins section (\fBtop-down\fP)

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-plugin': {}, // [0]
    'postcss\-plugin': {}, // [1]
    'postcss\-plugin': {}  // [2]
  }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.SS "Context"

.PP
When using a \fR{Function}\fP (\fRpostcss\&.config\&.js\fP or \fR\&.postcssrc\&.js\fP), it's possible to pass context to \fRpostcss-load-config\fP, which will be evaluated while loading your config\&. By default \fRctx\&.env (process\&.env\&.NODE_ENV)\fP and \fRctx\&.cwd (process\&.cwd())\fP are available on the \fRctx\fP \fR{Object}\fP

.PP
.RS 4
ℹ️ Most third-party integrations add additional properties to the \fRctx\fP (e\&.g \fRpostcss-loader\fP)\&. Check the specific module's README for more information about what is available on the respective \fRctx\fP 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.SS "Examples"

.PP
\fBpostcss\&.config\&.js\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = (ctx) => ({
  parser: ctx\&.parser ? 'sugarss' : false,
  map: ctx\&.env === 'development' ? ctx\&.map : false,
  plugins: {
    'postcss\-import': {},
    'postcss\-nested': {},
    cssnano: ctx\&.env === 'production' ? {} : false
  }
})
.fi
.PP

.PP
  

.PP
.PP
.nf
"scripts": {
  "build": "NODE_ENV=production node postcss",
  "start": "NODE_ENV=development node postcss"
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const { readFileSync } = require('fs')

const postcss = require('postcss')
const postcssrc = require('postcss\-load\-config')

const css = readFileSync('index\&.css', 'utf8')

const ctx = { parser: true, map: 'inline' }

postcssrc(ctx)\&.then(({ plugins, options }) => {
  postcss(plugins)
    \&.process(css, options)
    \&.then((result) => console\&.log(result\&.css))
})
.fi
.PP

.PP
  

.PP
.PP
.nf
"scripts": {
  "build": "NODE_ENV=production gulp",
  "start": "NODE_ENV=development gulp"
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const { task, src, dest, series, watch } = require('gulp')

const postcss = require('gulp\-postcssrc')

const css = () => {
  src('src/*\&.css')
    \&.pipe(postcss())
    \&.pipe(dest('dest'))
})

task('watch', () => {
  watch(['src/*\&.css', 'postcss\&.config\&.js'], css)
})

task('default', series(css, 'watch'))
.fi
.PP

.PP
  

.PP
.PP
.nf
"scripts": {
  "build": "NODE_ENV=production webpack",
  "start": "NODE_ENV=development webpack\-dev\-server"
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBwebpack\&.config\&.js\fP 
.PP
.nf
module\&.exports = (env) => ({
  module: {
    rules: [
      {
        test: /\\\&.css$/,
        use: [
          'style\-loader',
          'css\-loader',
          'postcss\-loader'
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
})

.fi
.PP

